WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.330
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.879
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:12.230
In the late 19th and early 20th century the
Japanese Empire was under rapid expansion,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.170
fueled by a feudal born military economy and
European technology.First invading the chain

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.370
of islands north and south of it’s home
island, fighting a bitter war with Russia

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:26.350
for control over Manchuria and annexing Korea
all before WW1.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.820
The outbreak of World War 1 gave Japan the
perfect platform to expand further, alling

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.020
against Germany and taking control of German
positions in the Pacific.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.000
Making Japan the dominant power in the east
Pacific, with its sphere of influence extending

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.980
from the Asian mainland to the mid Pacific.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.600
To maintain this influence Japan had undergone
rapid industrial expansion, growing from a

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.340
technologically primitive feudal country,
and developing the third largest Navy in the

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.090
world, with only the United States and the
British Navies challenging it.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.690
With US territory in the Philippines and Hawaii,
and British territory in Malaya, Hong Kong

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:04.960
and British Borneo, conflict was inevitable
and tension in the Pacific was rapidly growing.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.070
On September 27th 1940 Japan entered an alliance
with Germany and Italy, a clear warning to

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.070
the Americans.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.880
Enter the war in Europe, and you will face
war in the Pacific.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.070
This warning did not come unchallenged and
the United States moved it’s pacific fleet

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.120
from California to Pearl Harbour in Hawaii.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.729
From here many of us are familiar with the
story.

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:31.370
Japan capitalized on this ill advised show
of force and took the United States by surprise,

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:35.090
attempting to wipe out the US Navy in the
Pacific in one foul swoop.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.260
A decisive victory that announced Japan to
the Western world as a force to be reckoned

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.260
with.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.930
At the height of their power in WW2 Japan
seized control from Western powers in China

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.050
and Southeast Asia.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:51.150
But the war machine was doomed to failure,
and was destined to over extend itself.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.850
Nothing illustrates the turning fate of the
Japanese Empire like it’s iconic fighter

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.290
plane the Mitsubishi A6M Zero.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:02.479
A plane that entered world war 2 as feared
and formidable advisory for any plane in the

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:06.810
pacific theatre, but slowly and surely lost
its tactical advantage until it was finally

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:12.370
regulated to a dispensable resource, fitted
with 250 kilogram bombs and flown straight

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.989
at enemy ships in a last ditch effort to protect
the Empire.

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:18.319
The Zero was a thoughtfully designed plane.

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:23.859
Its designer Jiro Horikoshi, built upon the
framework of his previous design the A5M,

00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:28.999
the world’s first carrier based monoplane.The
Imperial Japanese Army challenged Mitsubishi

00:02:28.999 --> 00:02:34.709
and Nakajima to both design and build successors
to this plane to aid the war effort in China.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:41.359
The plane was to have a top speed of 500 km/h,
be fitted with two 7.7 mm machines guns and

00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:46.269
two 20 mm cannons, incredibly heavy armaments
for fighters of that era.

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:50.280
On top of all this, it was to have an operating
ceiling of 10,000 metres.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.950
Be capable of flying 2 hours at max speed,
and 6 to 8 hours at cruising speed with drop

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.379
tanks attached.

00:02:56.379 --> 00:03:00.109
These were fuel tanks that could be jettisoned
when empty, or when an enemy was encountered

00:03:00.109 --> 00:03:02.209
to increase maneuverability.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:06.260
These specifications alone were ambitious
enough, but the plane also needed to be carrier

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.400
based, which limited it’s wingspan to 12
metres.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.730
The specifications were so ambitious that
Nakajima pulled their bid from the project,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.809
but Jiro Horikoshi persevered.[1]

00:03:15.809 --> 00:03:17.840
His design [2] was ingenious.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.849
Incorporating many of the most advanced techniques
of the era.

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:25.389
Thin elliptical wings minimised drag, along
with state of the art flush riveting.

00:03:25.389 --> 00:03:29.200
New heat treatment knowledge obtained from
the Germans allowed Horikoshi to develop an

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.449
all metal structure.

00:03:30.449 --> 00:03:35.120
It’s frame entirely made from this new age
hardened aluminium and he cut holes into the

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.420
frame where possible to reduce weight.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.230
All to achieve that ambitious range requirement
of the Imperial Japanese Army, but that requirement

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.730
forced Jiro to make some sacrifices to the
planes design.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:50.030
Favouring speed, maneuverability and range
with its lightweight construction, over heavy

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.030
armoring.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:55.370
It’s skin was only 1.2 millimeters thick
over it’s thickest sections, like the leading

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.701
edge of the wing, and forward fuselage to
just 0.5 mm thick at it’s thinnest over

00:04:00.701 --> 00:04:02.769
the aft sections of the plane.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:07.540
While enemy outer skin thicknesses were not
significantly thicker, the outer skin is intended

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.439
to be a smooth aerodynamic surface not protective
armouring.

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:15.490
Typically these planes would contain thicker
plate armouring over key locations like the

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.850
engine, fuel tank and cockpit.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.440
The zero did not.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.170
On top of this the zero did not employ self-sealing
fuel tanks, which used several layers of rubber

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:29.320
that would swell and expand when soaked in
fuel, and thus seal any holes.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.570
Weight savings even came down to the cockpit
size, which was smaller than most Western

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.440
designs, designed to fit the on average, shorter
Japanese pilot.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.610
The zero was designed on a doctrine of training
skilled pilots with nimble, lightweight planes.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.480
What use was armor if you didn’t get hit,
what use was armor if you didn’t have the

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.340
range to patrol your territory.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.840
The lightweight skin made it difficult to
get into the plane without damaging it, so

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.570
the designers incorporated footholds and handles,
which sat flush to the planes surface, and

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.890
could be released with a button when needed.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.530
This mechanism seen from the inside of the
plane looks like a mushroom.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.480
With these rounded domes protecting an internal
bag which could be inflated using a pilot

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.630
controlled valve which redirected air from
the front air intake, so that in the event

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.090
of an crash landing into the sea the plane
would not sink.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.370
The designers of this plane clearly never
intended it to be used as a disposable weapon.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.590
Yet that is exactly what happened.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:25.970
The Japanese Air Force met little airborne
resistance in China, it’s vastly more advanced

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:30.100
planes picked the archaic chinese bi-planes
out of the sky with ease.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:35.310
It would meet a far more formidable enemy
on December 7th, 1941, when Japan attacked

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.390
Pearl Harbor.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:40.590
The surprise attack aimed to cripple the US
Navy and cement Japan as the most powerful

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.460
Navy in the Pacific.[3] A show of force to
dissuade the mighty American Navy from interfering

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.570
with the Empire’s domination of Asia.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.780
An ill advised attack that had the opposite
effect.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.800
America entered WW2 the same day, with it
two primary fighters at the time.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.832
The P-40 Warhawk, and the F4F Wildcat.

00:05:58.832 --> 00:06:03.650
Neither were as nimble as the A6M,and in early
battles the Zero had a tactical advantage,

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.060
despite both planes having a higher top speed.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.210
Much of this was down to the superb training
of the Japanese pilots, who at the outset

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.890
of the war, were among the best in the world.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.810
The Japanese focused on quality over quantity.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.460
This slowly changed over the course of the
war.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.970
Skilled pilots we dying quicker than Japan
could produce them, and instead Japan switched

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.060
it’s training process into overdrive.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.430
Sending hundreds of green pilots to war.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:32.210
The final blow to the legendary plane would
come when the US Navy captured an intact Zero

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.060
and began probing it for weaknesses.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.060
[4]

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:40.270
They soon discovered three fatal flaws that
would dismantle its tactical advantage overnight.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:44.940
The Americans knew of the Zero’s tendency
to burst into flames, due to it’s weak armouring

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.750
and lack of self sealing fuel tanks.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.420
It only took a few shots to disable the plane,
the trouble was getting into a position to

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.020
get those shots in.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.540
With American test flights of the Zero they
soon found their opening.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:01.100
The Zero suffered from the exact same problem
as early Merlin engines, which powered British

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.620
planes like the Spitfire and Hurricane,

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.901
It utilized a float carburetor, which as we
discovered in one of my early videos, made

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:11.690
zero-g maneuvers impossible as it caused the
float valve to open.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.710
Flooding the engine and shutting it off.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:17.780
Unlike the Merlin engines of the Spitfire
however, the Zero’s issues were never addressed,

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.440
and received little upgrades throughout the
war.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.330
The last nail in the coffin for the Zero came
with the discovery of it sluggish nature at

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:29.310
high speeds, becoming much less maneuverable
when maxed out, due to aerodynamic stiffening

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.380
of the ailerons.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:34.330
Where high airspeeds made it difficult for
pilots to extend the control surfaces with

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:38.590
their simple manual lever control system with
no help from hydraulics.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:42.630
With these characteristics in mind American
pilots were trained on how to deal with the

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:43.630
Zero.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:47.510
The primary rule being for Allied pilots to
maintain high speed and never to try to out-maneuver

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.370
a Zero at low speeds.

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:53.970
With its tactical advantage gone, and no improvements
to be seen, and Japan running short on skilled

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:58.850
pilots The Zero was given the role of Kamikaze
bomber, along with the other outdated planes

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.510
of the Japanese Air Force.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:05.230
For an island nation at war, running short
on valuable raw materials, this was a desperate

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.590
last attempt at protecting the motherland.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:11.720
The common story retold was that these young
men were brainwashed to an absolute devotion

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:12.850
to their emperor.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:14.570
The living god of Japan.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:18.930
All too often, detail is lost by only listening
to secondary sources.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:23.360
The stories told directly by Japanese pilots
paint a different story.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.430
Letters from young pilots [6], like Captain
Adachi Takuya, paint a picture of young men

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.860
sacrificing their lives for their loved ones.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.610
Perhaps the most telling of all stories comes
directly from the mouth of one of Japan’s

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.550
most famous aces “Saburo Sakai” . This
is a paraphrased quote from an interview before

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.139
his death in 2000.

00:08:40.139 --> 00:08:44.589
“A lot of Westerners looked at the kamikaze
strategy with complete shock, the idea of

00:08:44.589 --> 00:08:49.050
putting a kid in a plane and telling him to
kill himself by crashing into the enemy.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:53.279
But even if you don't tell him to crash into
something, putting a kid with only about 20

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:57.290
hours flight time into a plane and telling
him to take on U.S. pilots in Hellcats and

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:01.690
Corsairs is just as much a suicidal tactic
as being a kamikaze.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:05.749
We figured that if they're going to die anyway,
the kamikaze attack will probably cause more

00:09:05.749 --> 00:09:10.339
damage to the enemy for the same price in
lives.But let me tell you, all that stuff

00:09:10.339 --> 00:09:14.649
you read about "dying for the emperor ... Banzai!"
that's all crap.”

00:09:14.649 --> 00:09:18.600
It should be noted that in the same interview
[7] Saburo denies the Rape of Nanjing ever

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.649
happened, which is essentially on par with
holocaust denial, so you may want to take

00:09:22.649 --> 00:09:26.579
any information from this primary source with
a grain of salt.

00:09:26.579 --> 00:09:29.910
These young men were often barely trained
with more than a basic understanding of flight

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:34.970
mechanics, and many of them crashed into the
sea well away from enemy ships, precisely

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:38.680
because they never learned to deal with the
Zero’s control surfaces becoming essentially

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.470
useless at high speeds, like in a dive.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:45.529
Many of them died desperately pulling on their
stick, not being able to overcome the force

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:47.649
of air pushing the surface back down.

00:09:47.649 --> 00:09:52.170
I think it’s fair to say, these young men
were not solely dying out of duty for their

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.829
emperor, but for their family, their friends,
for their country.

00:09:55.829 --> 00:10:00.210
Emboldened by a culture where the group takes
precedence over the individual, where suicide

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:04.350
was viewed as a reasonable and honorable choice,
when faced with defeat.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.740
This wasn’t terrorism, as many western writers
have stated.This was war.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:14.080
The most devastating Kamikaze attack on May
11th 1945, came just two months after the

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.700
United States firebombed Tokyo, killing over
100,000, displacing 1 million and cutting

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.420
the industrial output of the city in half.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:26.589
This bombing raid was more destructive than
either of the nuclear bombs.

00:10:26.589 --> 00:10:31.769
With industrial output declining, raw materials
scarce, trained men few and far between and

00:10:31.769 --> 00:10:33.470
their homeland under attack.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.939
This culture of self sacrifice for the country
made Japan a fortress that no army wanted

00:10:37.939 --> 00:10:39.149
to invade.

00:10:39.149 --> 00:10:43.769
Made worse by President Roosevelt’s policy
of unconditional surrender, which only encouraged

00:10:43.769 --> 00:10:50.319
unconditional resistance, and so two young
Japanese Pilots Kiyoshi Ogawa and Seizo Yasunori,

00:10:50.319 --> 00:10:55.839
each piloting Zeros with 250 kg bombs attached
flew to their death on that day.

00:10:55.839 --> 00:11:00.360
Emerging from low cloud cover the two rookie
pilots began their diving attack on the aircraft

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.889
carrier the USS Bunker Hill, releasing their
bombs and proceeding to crash their planes

00:11:04.889 --> 00:11:06.639
straight into the flight deck.

00:11:06.639 --> 00:11:10.649
Both bombs penetrate d the flight deck and
the resulting fires and explosions claimed

00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:13.499
the lives of over 390 sailors and airmen.

00:11:13.499 --> 00:11:18.059
A devastating attack, that disabled the aircraft
carrier for the rest of the war, but this

00:11:18.059 --> 00:11:23.199
was a rare occurrence and the vast majority
kamikaze attacks failed to hit a target.

00:11:23.199 --> 00:11:27.800
Either missing the target completely, or being
intercepted by anti-aircraft fire and enemy

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:28.800
fighters.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.250
Ultimately the Japanese were being pushed
back to their native Island and the B-29s

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:37.269
that dropped the nuclear bombs in Nagasaki
and Hiroshima weren’t even escorted over

00:11:37.269 --> 00:11:42.010
Japanese land, as the Japanese Air Force had
little resources to counter an attack like

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.010
this.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.509
Their airforce had risen and fallen alongside
their empire.

